ion/math-academy-id "q-237021" :question/problem "Let $c=⟨-3,\\sqrt{3},1⟩$ and $d=⟨x,0,-12⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $c$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $d$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\nc⊥d\\,⟹\\,c\\cdot d=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & ={c}_{x}\\cdot {d}_{x}+{c}_{y}\\cdot {d}_{y}+{c}_{z}\\cdot {d}_{z} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot x+\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0+1\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-3x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & =0 \\\\ -3x-12 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-12 \\\\ x & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-237021/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51e317c-f6b3-5704-afb4-bdfc79f1176f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-237021"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-318981/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "0ed73de5-a5a8-545e-afd0-15dc790f5862" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-318981/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "f9851f89-5e9d-513d-9368-95fd4360bd6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-318981/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8528824e-b9e0-5132-873e-783fe728400c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-318981/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-318981/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-318981/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-318981" :question/id #uuid "3ce822af-94cc-52ef-a9f7-5da5b1ed7557" :question/math-academy-id "q-318981" :question/problem "Let $r=⟨-1,2,3⟩$ and $s=⟨2,0,0⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $r$ onto $s.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{s}\\,r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{s}\\,r & =\\frac{r\\cdot s}{|\\,s\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{r}_{x}{s}_{x}+{r}_{y}{s}_{y}+{r}_{z}{s}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{s}_{x}^{2}+{s}_{y}^{2}+{s}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)(2)+(2)(0)+(3)(0)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-318981/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91691448-c59a-56dc-a775-d4e9c3297036"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-318981"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-319150/field-1/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" :answer/id #uuid "71d29705-2346-55b4-8fc0-c4d3f6aa8ae8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-319150/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "54676cc8-2bd5-55d1-8dd6-b1ebac444a4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-319150/field-1/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" :answer-field/choices ["q-319150/field-1/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-319150" :question/id #uuid "ef00ed14-3eea-52f8-8f98-16b6f55b0077" :question/math-academy-id "q-319150" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨3,-1,2⟩$ and $q=⟨x,1,1⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 3x+1 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-319150/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51e317c-f6b3-5704-afb4-bdfc79f1176f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-319150"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
